## Properties, not public fields

```csharp
public class Product
{
    private decimal _price;                       // a field: storage
    public decimal Price                          // a property: controlled access
    {
        get => _price;
        set => _price = value >= 0 ? value : throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(value));
    }
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";        // auto-property with an initialiser
    public string Sku { get; init; } = "";        // settable only during initialisation
    public required string Category { get; init; } // must be set by the creator (C# 11)
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; } = DateTime.UtcNow; // get-only: set here or in the constructor
}
```

Properties let you add validation or change the storage later without changing callers. Public fields are only appropriate for small structs and constants.

An auto-property such as `Name { get; set; }` compiles to a hidden field and two small methods, which the JIT inlines, so it costs nothing at runtime. The difference is the contract: an interface can declare a property but not a field, and turning a public field into a property later is a breaking change for assemblies already compiled against it.

## Constructors

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IOrderRepository _repo;
    public OrderService(IOrderRepository repo) => _repo = repo;
}

// C# 12 primary constructor: the parameter is in scope for the whole class
public class OrderService(IOrderRepository repo)
{
    public Task<Order?> Find(int id) => repo.FindAsync(id);
}
```

`readonly` fields can be assigned only in the declaration or a constructor. Primary-constructor parameters are captured, mutable variables, not readonly fields; assign them to a `readonly` field if that matters. A method that writes `repo = other;` compiles, and every later call then uses the new value. A record is different: there a positional parameter becomes a public init-only property.

## static

Static members belong to the type, not to instances: `Counter.Count`. Static state is shared by every request in a web app, so mutable statics are a concurrency and test-isolation hazard; keep statics for pure helpers, constants and factory methods. The classic case is a static `List<Order>` used as a cache: two requests calling `Add` at the same moment can corrupt the list, and a test that fills it leaks orders into the next test. Register a singleton service holding a `ConcurrentDictionary` instead.

## Records

```csharp
public record Money(decimal Amount, string Currency);

var a = new Money(1m, "EUR");
var b = new Money(1m, "EUR");
a == b                     // true: value equality over all members
a.ToString()               // "Money { Amount = 1, Currency = EUR }"
var c = a with { Amount = 2m };   // non-destructive copy with one change
var (amount, currency) = a;       // positional records deconstruct
```

A positional record gets init-only properties, value-based `Equals` / `GetHashCode` / `==`, a readable `ToString`, `Deconstruct` and `with` support. You can add members and validation to a record body.

Equality compares every member with that member's own `Equals`, so a record holding a `List<string>` compares the list by reference: two records with lists of equal contents are not equal. A record body may also redeclare a positional member as a settable property. That compiles, but changing a record after it went into a `HashSet` changes its hash code, and the set no longer finds it.

## class, record or struct?

| type | equality | typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `class` | reference | entities with identity and lifecycle (an Order you load and change), services |
| `record` (class) | value | DTOs, commands, events, value objects (Money, Address), API results |
| `struct` / `record struct` | value, copied | tiny immutable values (coordinates, ids) where allocation matters |

Two `Customer` class instances with the same fields are NOT `==`; two equal records are. Do not use records for EF Core entities that are tracked and mutated: their value equality and immutability fight the change tracker (topic 19).

## Encapsulation for domain objects

Expose behaviour, not setters: `order.AddLine(sku, qty)` can enforce "no more than 50 lines" and "cannot change a shipped order"; a public `List<Line> Lines { get; set; }` cannot. Expose collections as `IReadOnlyList<T>`.

A common shape keeps `private readonly List<Line> _lines = new();` and exposes `public IReadOnlyList<Line> Lines => _lines;`. `AddLine` checks the count and the order's status before it adds, so callers can read and loop but cannot change the lines without the checks. A caller can still cast the view back to `List<Line>`; return `_lines.AsReadOnly()` when that matters.

