// ──────────────────────────── types ──────────────────────────────────────

record Money(long Cents, string Currency)
{
    public Money Add(Money other)
    {
        if (other.Currency != Currency) throw new InvalidOperationException($"cannot add {other.Currency} to {Currency}");
        return this with { Cents = Cents + other.Cents };
    }

    public Money Times(int factor) => this with { Cents = Cents * factor };

    public override string ToString() =>
        (Cents / 100m).ToString("0.00", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " " + Currency;
}

drills.explanation:
`with` builds the new values without restating every member, and the invariant culture pins the decimal separator:

```csharp
public Money Add(Money other)
{
    if (other.Currency != Currency) throw new InvalidOperationException($"cannot add {other.Currency} to {Currency}");
    return this with { Cents = Cents + other.Cents };
}

public override string ToString() =>
    (Cents / 100m).ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " " + Currency;
```

Dividing by `100m` (a decimal) keeps the cents exact. `Cents / 100` is integer division on a `long`, so the test "ToString uses two decimals and the currency" gets `12.00` for 1234 cents, and the boundary test gets `0.00` for -5. Changing `Cents` in place instead of returning a copy fails "Add returns the sum and leaves the operands alone", because the left operand turns into the sum. Overriding `ToString` does not affect the generated equality.

